---
import "../styles/global.css";

const basePath = import.meta.env.BASE_URL.replace(/\/?$/, "/");
const scoutExample = JSON.stringify({
  war_summary: "FIELD DESK · Day 113 — Oarbreaker remains the busiest front...",
  selected_regions: ["OarbreakerHex", "ClahstraHex", "MooringCountyHex"],
}, null, 2);
const forecastExample = JSON.stringify({
  predictions: [{
    rank: 1,
    base_id: "OarbreakerHex:example",
    outcome: "CAPTURED_BY_COLONIALS",
    eta_utc: "2026-08-24T08:15:00Z",
    confidence: 0.62,
    sigma_minutes: 45,
    evidence: [{ metric_id: "region.OarbreakerHex.totalEnlistments.rate_1h_per_hour", relevance: 8 }],
  }],
}, null, 2);
const adviceExample = JSON.stringify({
  strategic_advice: {
    colonial_reinforce: {
      base_id: "OarbreakerHex:example",
      reason: "Recent pressure and ownership changes make this a fragile point.",
      evidence: [{ metric_id: "region.OarbreakerHex.wardenCasualties.rate_1h_per_hour", relevance: 7 }],
    },
  },
}, null, 2);
---

<!doctype html>
<html lang="en">
  <head>
    <meta charset="UTF-8" />
    <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width" />
    <meta name="description" content="A beginner-friendly walkthrough of the Foxhole Forecast prompt and evidence flow." />
    <title>Prompt Flow · Foxhole Forecast</title>
  </head>
  <body>
    <header class="method-masthead">
      <div class="masthead__eyebrow">PROMPT FLOW · SAME CUTOFF, FROZEN EVIDENCE, OBSERVED RESULTS</div>
      <div class="method-hero page-width">
        <div>
          <p class="kicker">Inside one forecast round</p>
          <h1>From scout packet<br /><span>to settlement</span></h1>
          <p class="sublede">This is the data path behind a round. It separates what a model saw, what it recommended, what it predicted, and what the public API later observed.</p>
        </div>
        <a class="method-back" href={basePath}>← Return to dashboard</a>
      </div>
    </header>

    <nav class="section-nav" aria-label="Prompt flow navigation">
      <div class="page-width">
        <a href={basePath}>Live dashboard</a>
        <a href={`${basePath}predictions/`}>Round history</a>
        <a href={`${basePath}history/`}>Summary archive</a>
      </div>
    </nav>

    <main class="prompt-flow-page page-width">
      <section class="method-intro prompt-flow-intro">
        <p class="kicker">The basic idea</p>
        <p>Every model gets a time-stamped snapshot, not a live feed. It first writes a short war-desk dispatch and chooses regions worth inspecting. It then receives detailed history for those regions and makes eight timed predictions plus four separate strategic recommendations.</p>
        <aside class="method-callout"><strong>Important distinction</strong><span>The eight predictions are scored. Recommendations are neutral, unscored adviser notes. Players did not see or act on them, so later activity cannot prove that a recommendation caused anything.</span></aside>
      </section>

      <section class="flow-section" aria-labelledby="flow-title">
        <div class="scoring-section__heading"><p class="kicker">01–07</p><h2 id="flow-title">One round, step by step</h2></div>
        <div class="flow-timeline">
          <article class="flow-step"><span>01</span><div><h3>Scout packet</h3><p>At a shared UTC cutoff, the model sees the public whole-map packet: ownership, recent base events, casualty pace, and enlistment activity.</p><small>Same cutoff for every participating model.</small></div></article>
          <article class="flow-step"><span>02</span><div><h3>Newspaper summary + region choices</h3><p>The scout writes a compact, neutral newspaper-style summary and selects up to six regions whose detailed history might help with base-ownership forecasts.</p><small>This summary is archived as a model briefing.</small></div></article>
          <article class="flow-step"><span>03</span><div><h3>Detailed packet</h3><p>The model receives deeper, cutoff-safe history for exactly those selected regions. It does not get to add facts from after the cutoff.</p><small>Region selection is itself part of what we can inspect.</small></div></article>
          <article class="flow-step"><span>04</span><div><h3>Eight timed predictions</h3><p>The model names eight different strategic bases, what should happen, an exact UTC ETA, a chance, a timing spread, and cited evidence.</p><small>Ranks 1–4 target the next six hours; ranks 5–8 target 6–24 hours after cutoff.</small></div></article>
          <article class="flow-step"><span>05</span><div><h3>Four strategic recommendations</h3><p>It also suggests one base for each neutral prompt: Colonial reinforce, Colonial attack, Warden reinforce, and Warden attack. These are advisory notes, not scoreable bets.</p><small>Recommendations carry their own reason and evidence.</small></div></article>
          <article class="flow-step"><span>06</span><div><h3>Evidence frozen</h3><p>The prediction, recommendation, cutoff, evidence IDs, and model response are retained. Later information cannot be edited into the original call.</p><small>That is what makes a forecast prospective rather than hindsight.</small></div></article>
          <article class="flow-step"><span>07</span><div><h3>15-minute observations and settlement</h3><p>We continue polling the official API. Ownership transitions are observed in intervals, then the eight predictions are scored against those observations and their deadlines.</p><small>Recommendations can be compared descriptively with later enlistment, casualty, and ownership activity—but not treated as causal.</small></div></article>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="prompt-example-section" aria-labelledby="examples-title">
        <div class="scoring-section__heading"><p class="kicker">Illustrative, trimmed examples</p><h2 id="examples-title">What the model actually returns</h2></div>
        <p class="method-caption">The examples below show the shape of the exchange with fictional values. The forecast response contains both the predictions and strategic-advice blocks shown separately here. They are intentionally shortened; the exact prompt files remain available in the repository.</p>
        <div class="prompt-example-grid">
          <details class="prompt-example" open>
            <summary>Scout response</summary>
            <pre><code>{scoutExample}</code></pre>
          </details>
          <details class="prompt-example">
            <summary>Forecast response</summary>
            <pre><code>{forecastExample}</code></pre>
          </details>
          <details class="prompt-example">
            <summary>Strategic recommendations</summary>
            <pre><code>{adviceExample}</code></pre>
          </details>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="prompt-access-section" aria-labelledby="prompt-access-title">
        <div class="scoring-section__heading"><p class="kicker">Full source text</p><h2 id="prompt-access-title">Read the exact prompts</h2></div>
        <p>The live page keeps examples readable, while the repository remains the source of truth for the full markdown, including output rules and validation constraints.</p>
        <div class="prompt-links"><a href="https://github.com/fh-eval/foxhole-forecast/blob/main/prompts/scout.md" rel="noreferrer">Open full scout prompt ↗</a><a href="https://github.com/fh-eval/foxhole-forecast/blob/main/prompts/forecast.md" rel="noreferrer">Open full forecast prompt ↗</a><a href="https://github.com/fh-eval/foxhole-forecast/tree/main/prompts" rel="noreferrer">Browse all prompt versions ↗</a></div>
        <p class="method-caption">The prompt asks for facts supported by the packet. It does not grant access to player communications, hidden inventories, or an in-game computer-use agent.</p>
      </section>
    </main>

    <footer><div class="page-width"><strong>FOXHOLE FORECAST</strong><a href={basePath}>Live dashboard</a></div></footer>
  </body>
</html>
